# 5 Four worked examples {#ch:examples}

Each example below is a complete program in the `examples/` folder
next to this guide. All four were compiled with
`qcc -O2 -Wall -fopenmp` and run for this guide (3 OpenMP threads on a
desktop machine); the figures show their actual output. Compile and run any
of them with

~~~bash
qcc -O2 -Wall -fopenmp cavity.c -o cavity -lm
OMP_NUM_THREADS=4 ./cavity 2> log.txt
~~~

## Lid-driven cavity {#sec:ex-cavity}

A square box of unit side full of fluid; the top wall slides at speed 1.
With $\nu = 10^{-3}$ the Reynolds number is 1000 and the flow settles into
one large vortex with smaller ones in the bottom corners. This is the
standard first test of an incompressible solver.

~~~literatec
~~~

**What to notice.**

- `grid/multigrid.h` gives a uniform $64\times64$ grid without
  adaptation.

- Only the tangential velocity needs a condition: the normal component
  is zero on every wall by default ([chapter 18](18_boundary)). The `uf.n` lines set the
  face velocity on the walls directly, which `$BASILISK/test/lid.c` notes
  improves the result with the centered solver.

- `mu[] = {1e-3, 1e-3}` makes the viscosity a constant face
  field ([chapter 2](02_language)).

- The `logfile` event has an end time (`t <= 60`); without it
  the run would stop after one step, because no event would have an upper
  limit ([section 2.5](02_language#sec:events)). The event also stops the run early by returning
  1 once $\max|u - u_{\text{prev}}| < 10^{-4}$ over 0.5 time units.

**Result.** The run reached steady state at $t = 41$ after 2933
steps, in about 4 seconds. Figure 5.1 shows the vorticity and the
horizontal velocity on the vertical centre line. The minimum, $u = -0.382$
at $y = -0.325$, is the value usually compared with the benchmark
solutions of Ghia, Ghia and Shin (1982).

![Figure 5.1: Lid-driven cavity at $Re = 1000$, $64\times64$ cells, steady state.
Left: vorticity (colour range $\pm5$). Right: horizontal velocity along the
vertical centre line.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_cavity.png)

## Flow past a cylinder: a vortex street {#sec:ex-karman}

Uniform flow at $U_0 = 1$ enters a channel and passes a cylinder of
diameter $D = 0.125$; with $\nu = U_0D/160$ the Reynolds number is 160 and
the wake becomes unsteady and sheds vortices. The program is the
`$BASILISK/examples/karman.c` example with the drag and lift added.

~~~literatec
~~~

**What to notice.**

- `embed.h` is included before the solver; `solid()` in
  `init` builds the cylinder; `u.n[embed]` and `u.t[embed]` make
  it no-slip ([chapter 19](19_embed)).

- The viscosity is multiplied by `fm.x[]` in `properties`,
  so it is zero on solid faces.

- Inflow and outflow come in pairs: velocity and `neumann` pressure
  on the left, `neumann` velocity and `dirichlet` pressure on the
  right, for both `p` and `pf` ([section 18.7](18_boundary#sec:wellposed)).

- `adapt_wavelet()` on `cs` and `u` keeps the cylinder
  and the wake at level 9 and the rest coarse: about 4400 cells instead of
  the 262 144 of a uniform level-9 grid.

- `embed_force()` gives the pressure and viscous forces (2D only).

**Result.** 3000 steps to $t = 15$ took 30 seconds. After the start
the drag coefficient oscillates between 1.30 and 1.38 (mean 1.34) and the
lift between $\pm0.55$, with a period of 0.63, so the Strouhal number is
$St = D/(U_0T) = 0.20$ (figure 5.2).

![Figure 5.2: Flow past a cylinder at $Re = 160$. Top: vorticity at $t = 15$
(range $\pm10$; the cylinder is at the left). Bottom: drag and lift
coefficients.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_karman.png)

## A rising bubble {#sec:ex-bubble}

A circular bubble of radius 0.25 (density 100, viscosity 1) rises in a
heavier liquid (density 1000, viscosity 10) under gravity 0.98, with
surface tension 24.5. This is the first test case of Hysing et al. (2009),
and `$BASILISK/test/rising.c` runs it with many variants. Here gravity
points along $-x$, so the bubble rises to the right.

~~~literatec
~~~

**What to notice.**

- `dimensions (nx = 4)` on a multigrid makes a box four times
  longer than wide ([chapter 3](03_setup)): $[0,2]\times[0,0.5]$. The bubble is
  centred on $y = 0$, so only its upper half is computed; the default
  free-slip wall at $y = 0$ is a symmetry plane.

- `two-phase.h` provides `f`, `rho1` ... `mu2`;
  `tension.h` adds surface tension through `f.sigma`; `reduced.h`
  adds gravity through `G` and `Z` ([chapter 14](14_acceleration)).

- The bubble is fluid 2 ($f = 0$), so its volume, centroid and velocity
  are integrals weighted by $1 - f$, computed with reductions.

- `t = {0, 1, 2, 3}` schedules the shape output at listed times.

**Result.** The run took under 3 seconds. The rise velocity peaks
at 0.242 at $t = 0.92$ and the centroid reaches $x = 1.079$ at $t = 3$;
the bubble flattens into an ellipse (figure 5.3).

![Figure 5.3: Rising bubble, $128\times32$ cells. Left: interface at
$t = 0, 1, 2, 3$ from `output_facets()`; the whole width of the box
is shown, and the lower edge $y = 0$ is the symmetry plane. Right: rise
velocity of the bubble.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_bubble.png)

## A collapsing water column {#sec:ex-dambreak}

A column of water 0.25 m wide and 0.5 m high stands in the corner of a
1 m box filled with air, and collapses under gravity. This example uses
real fluid properties (SI units), a density ratio of 833, and adaptive
refinement on the interface.

~~~literatec
~~~

**What to notice.**

- With $\rho_1/\rho_2 = 833$, `navier-stokes/conserving.h` moves
  momentum with the volume fraction ([chapter 12](12_momentum)). For this guide the
  same program was also run without that line. It did not blow up, but it
  needed 2650 steps instead of 750 (the time step fell to
  $3.7\times10^{-5}$ s instead of about $5\times10^{-4}$ s) and adaptation
  created up to 15 000 cells instead of 4700, because spurious air velocities
  near the interface both limit the CFL step and trigger refinement.

- `fraction (f, min (0.25 - x, 0.5 - y))` builds the rectangle as
  the intersection of two half-planes.

- All walls are the default free-slip walls; no boundary condition is
  written.

- The front position is a `reduction(max:xf)` over cells near the
  floor that are mostly water.

**Result.** 750 steps to $t = 0.6$ took 8 seconds with 3000 to 4700
cells. The front reaches the far wall at $t \approx 0.33$, then the water
runs up the wall (figure 5.4).

![Figure 5.4: Collapsing water column, maximum level 7. Left: the interface at
four times. At $t = 0$ the column's edges lie exactly on cell faces, so every
cell is full or empty and `output_facets()` writes nothing; the initial
rectangle is drawn directly. Right: position of the front along the floor.](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/a-k-sangwan/basilisk-guide/main/basilisk_guide/figures/fig_dambreak.png)

## From the sandbox: a wave growing on a sheared interface {#sec:ex-shear}

This case is not one of the four programs above and was not run for this
guide. It is the author's sandbox case
[sandbox/aksangwan/shear_flow](/sandbox/aksangwan/shear_flow/), shown here because
it uses several techniques the other examples do not: an initial condition
computed by an external program, an interface built from a list of points,
and restarting from a dump file.

A lighter fluid ($\rho_2/\rho_1 = 0.5$) lies over a heavier one at rest, in
a periodic box one wavelength wide ($\lambda = 2\pi/k = 21.5$ cm, CGS units,
$g = 980$, $\sigma = 72$, both fluids inviscid). The top wall moves at
$U_0 = 199.6$ cm/s and the upper fluid has the exponential profile
$U(y) = U_0(1 - e^{-y/b})$ with $b = 2.54$ cm. The interface starts as
$\eta = a\cos kx$, $a = 0.0685$ cm, with the velocity of the corresponding
unstable mode of linear theory, whose complex wave speed is
$c = 61.26 + 22.55\,i$ cm/s. In the linear stage the amplitude should
therefore grow like $e^{k c_i t}$ with $k c_i = 6.59$ s$^{-1}$, which is the
first thing to check in the output.

~~~literatec
u.n[top]    = dirichlet(0.);
u.n[bottom] = dirichlet(0.);
u.t[top]    = dirichlet(U0);    // moving wall drives the shear
u.t[bottom] = neumann(0.);
...
periodic (right);
G.y = -gG;
f.sigma = 72.;
rho1 = 1.0; rho2 = density_ratio*rho1;
mu1 = 0.;   mu2 = 0.;
~~~

**What to notice.**

- The velocity
  of the linear mode involves hypergeometric functions of complex argument,
  which are easier to evaluate in Python (`scipy.special.hyp2f1`). The
  `init` event writes the centre of every cell to a file with
  `foreach()`, calls `genData.py` through `system()`, then
  reads the velocities back in a second `foreach()` in the same order.
  This works because both loops visit the cells in the same order, which is
  true only in a serial run. With `-fopenmp`, `qcc` refuses to
  compile a `foreach()` that increments a shared counter. A version that
  also runs in parallel would have Python tabulate the profiles $\hat u(y)$
  and $\hat v(y)$ once on a fine one-dimensional grid, and `init` would
  interpolate them at each cell and multiply by $e^{ikx}$.

- `distance.h`
  reads the points $(x, \eta(x))$ with `input_xy()` and computes the
  signed distance to that curve at every cell centre. The program averages
  it to the vertices and calls `fractions (phi, f)`, as in
  [chapter 17](17_vof). `distance.h` works only in serial.

- `./run -r 20` sets the dump file name
  before `run()`. The `init` event tries `restore()` first
  and only builds the initial condition if that fails, which is the usual
  Basilisk pattern for restarts ([chapter 3](03_setup)).

- The adaptation criterion includes the
  curvature from `curvature (f, KAPPA)`. `curvature()` sets
  `KAPPA` to `nodata` away from the interface, and
  `adapt_wavelet()` has no special case for `nodata`, so the jump
  between a defined curvature and `nodata` counts as a large error.
  Only interface cells have a defined curvature, and the `f` criterion
  already refines those, so the curvature criterion mainly matters where the
  curvature itself varies quickly along the interface.

## Where to go next

Change one thing at a time and look at what happens: raise the level and
check whether the result changes (convergence), switch off
`conserving.h` in the dam break and watch the time step, set a limiter
(`u.x.gradient = minmod2`) in the cavity, or add a passive tracer with
`tracer.h` to the Kármán street as in the original example.

